  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 PM EST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis and Forecast:\n\nLarge trough complex currently resides over the Front Range/High \nPlains with associated elongated, or soon to be elongating, sfc low \npressure system across the Plains. Nice scoop of column moisture \nfrom this system being advected northward, quite anomalous for this \ntime of year. As the trough complex moves eastward with time, this \nmoisture goes with it, beginning tonight and remaining into mid \nweek.\n\nSfc cyclone generally moves northeast with time tonight in response \nto the northern piece of energy within this troughing complex. \nMeanwhile, a secondary cyclone begins to take shape across the C \nPlains-ish area due to the main upper low's height falls. Given the \ntrack of the low pressure and subsequent upper divergence, northern \nLake Michigan and adjacent eastern UP will see the best coverage and \nhealth of precipitation tonight. There is some potential for \n>0.5\" of rainfall overnight, which will be relatively efficient\nrainfall given the anomalous moisture. As the main trough \ncontinues northeastward and secondary low pressure system \nfollows a similar trajectory as the first, the forcing will \nremain, at least temporarily, best across the E UP with \nadditional shower and rain chances. Instability profiles are \nextremely meager given the saturated profiles and lack of \nhealthy dews at the sfc. While a brief convective nature to some\nof this activity cannot be ruled out, especially later tonight \ninto Tuesday across the E UP, this will only produce locally \nhigher rainfall totals. Nevertheless, through this period, \ntonight through Tuesday night, the E UP could see 0.5 to 1.0\"+ \nof rainfall, which should be spread out enough to be beneficial \n(D2 drought currently in E UP). Generally, rainfall amounts \ntaper the farther southeast one goes across northern lower MI \ndue to lack of forcing. Could only see a tenth of two or less \nacross much of northern lower MI. Breezy conditions are \nanticipated on Tuesday as well, especially near and west of \nI-75, and more particularly, the Lake Michigan shoreline. \n\nAs we head into Wednesday, cyclone and upper low mature over the \nUpper Midwest and slowly move east, and precipitation chances will \ncontinue and spread over much of northern lower MI and eastern upper \nMI through the day. Rainfall amounts are progged to be relatively \nlight, despite the impressive pattern overall as the upper low moves \nover the area. Looks like we could almost be in no mans land to a \ndegree over northern lower, where the best upper forcing does not \nmarry with the low level lift, and thus the lack of true, widespread \nwetting rains. Instability profiles look meager at best with warm \ntemps aloft as well. Nevertheless, showers are still expected across \nthe area, moving west to east, within a moist environment, as the \nupper low migrates eastward. Any subtleties with a progged short wave \nrotating through the main upper low could change the precipitation\nfootprint as well, so worth keeping an eye on in regards to \nrain chances. Throughout this entire time, temperatures will run\nwell above average for early November, with widespread mid to \nupper 60s, to perhaps low 70s, for daytime highs both Tuesday \nand Wednesday. \n\nAs the parent upper low meanders to the east late week, another \npiece of energy dives into its backside and right over northern MI. \nSo, despite the best moisture evacuating to the east Thursday and \nFriday, this reinvigorated energy will keep showers in the forecast \nlate week. We watch another system for this weekend diving out of \nthe northwest. Current forecast cools temps back down to seasonable, \nor slightly above.",
